the type's constructors split into rendered, carrier, composite and unread: true
a constructor of another shape is unread, and refused: true
a nullary constructor mints the tag its renderer case returns: true
the renderer must return a carrier's string unchanged: true
a composite renders each of its provenances, in order, through the renderer: true
a composite dropping or reordering a provenance is refused: true
a nullary constructor with no rendering is refused: true
a missing type is refused rather than read as no family: true
a carrier applied to no literal anywhere is the reader gone blind: true
every tag literal in a relaying exception's scope is a code, a helper's argument included: true
a code belongs to the function declaring its exception: true
the relaying family is named after its exception and its carrier: true
a carrier applied to a literal mints it, qualified or not, inside a scope or outside: true
a tag in a comment is minted by no family: true
an exception whose payload no handler hands to a carrier opens no family: true
a helper inside a scope mints for the scope, and a scope declared anew inside it is its own: true
a same-named scope whose own handler does not relay mints nothing: true
a handler whose carrier receives some other value than the payload relays nothing: true
the same handler shape relays when the carrier does receive the payload: true
a payload wrapped in a result constructor is relayed by a caller matching it into a carrier: true
a result constructor no caller hands to a carrier relays nothing: true
a caller of a same-named function of another module relays nothing: true
a caller qualifying the declaring module through a binding relays: true
a caller qualifying the declaring module directly relays: true
the relaying exception applied to a string that is no tag is refused, not skipped: true
a string raised through an exception that relays nothing is no provenance: true
a function declaring no exception adds no code to a source that does: true
the exception with the number cites the code: true
a citation wrapped at the line break still cites it: true
the tag itself cites it, in prose or in a string: true
the word provenance with a number, emphasized or not, cites any family's tag: true
a tag continued by another tag character, or by a longer number, is a different tag: true
an identifier ending in a spelling word is not that word: true
the exception with no number, or with a variable, cites nothing: true
a longer number is not a prefix match on a shorter code: true
an exception spelling cites only its own family's numbers: true
a family whose last code is retired still has its citations read, and refused: true
a word-number of a number no tag has is stale: true
a bare numeral is not read: true
a tag-shaped token no source mints is a stale citation: a retired tag in prose: true
a test's own tag is a known citation, not a stale one: true
colon-joined text that is not a tag is not read: true
the table reads as its literal pairs: true
a table with an element of another shape is unread, not guessed at: true
a source with no table binding has no table: true
a table agreeing with the minting functions is accepted: true
a table entry placed in the other function's phase is refused: true
a table entry no raise site mints is refused, a tag of another family included: true
a table entry naming a phase with no minter is refused: true
an unreadable table is refused rather than skipped: true
a phase whose function mints nothing is stale: true
a fixture library citing its own tags is accepted: true
a carrier applied to a string that is no tag is recorded, and refused: true
a module declaring its own string constructor of a carrier's name is foreign: true
a foreign constructor reached through a chain of module bindings mints nothing: true
a module name resolves to the binding in scope where it is used: true
a local structure declaring its own carrier-named constructor is foreign, inside and out: true
a foreign constructor applied unqualified in its own module mints nothing: true
a foreign constructor qualified through an alias mints nothing; the owner's still does: true
a constructor's rendering minted by any other family is refused: true
a new number minted under two tags is refused: true
a pinned collision is accepted: true
a third tag on a pinned number is refused: true
a pin that no longer collides is stale: true
a test's tag may reuse a library number: true
a test constructing a tag no library source mints, not spelled as a test tag, is refused: true
a test citing a library tag through the carrier is no mint of its own: true
a library tag spelled as a test tag is refused: true
a minted tag with a one-word reason is refused: true
a code minted in two functions is refused: its provenance cannot name the phase: true
a stale citation is refused with its file: true
a minting source whose tags the citation reader misses is the reader gone blind: true
an excluded prefix no file lives under is stale: true
shipping inventory lists a page citing tags all three ways: true
shipping inventory derives the families from the type: true
shipping inventory groups the codes under the function minting them: true
shipping inventory refuses a citation of a code no raise site mints: true
shipping inventory refuses a prose citation of a retired tag: true
shipping inventory refuses a new number collision: true
shipping inventory reads a relaying source that never spells the carrier: true
shipping inventory refuses a phase table placing a code in the wrong phase: true
